Thus concludes the <s>Sādhana-Dīpikā</s>, second section (<s>kakṣā</s>),
uttered by <s>Rādhākṛṣṇadāsa</s>, the humble follower of the feet of <s>Śrī Haridāsa Gosvāmī</s>, the chief of the service of <s>Śrī Govinda Deva</s>.
//2//

'The paternal affection of <s>Purī Gosāñi</s> is foremost; the pure friendship of <s>Rāmānanda</s>;
the pure servitorship of <s>Govinda</s> and others in the <s>dāsya-rasa</s>;
<s>Gadādhara</s>, <s>Jagadānanda</s>, <s>Svarūpa</s>'s chief delightful <s>rasa</s> —
by these four moods, the Lord is under control.'

Commentary: 'In the desired object' means in the object of one's longed-for <s>prema</s>, that is, in <s>Śrī Nanda-nandana</s> (<s>Kṛṣṇa</s>). '<s>Svārasikī</s>' means natural;
'supreme absorption' means bodily, verbal, and mental activities. That is <s>rāga</s>. '<s>Tan-mayī</s>' means impelled solely by that;
the devotion which is such is called '<s>rāgātmikā</s>' — thus the syntactical connection. 'Deep thirst consisting of <s>prema</s> toward the desired object' — this is
the essential definition. 'Natural supreme absorption in the desired object' — this is the marginal definition.

Those whose activities transgress the path of propriety due to the exuberance of passionate love, yet who are worshiped by the host of chaste women headed by <s>Arundhatī</s> who are radiant with exalted faith;
though dwelling in the forest, whose sweetness and fragrance eclipse the splendor of <s>Lakṣmī</s> —
may those companions of <s>Kṛṣṇa</s>, extraordinary in all three worlds, grant you bliss!

Obeisance to <s>Caitanya Deva</s>, who serves His own name-nectar;
who, by taking shelter of <s>Rūpa</s> — and so forth.
Now, the venerable <s>Jīva Gosvāmī</s> was the nephew (brother's son) of the venerable <s>Rūpa</s>, and therefore [<s>Rūpa</s>] made him his <s>mantra</s>-initiated disciple. But he (<s>Jīva</s>) did not have the direct audience (<s>darśana</s>) of <s>Śrīman Mahāprabhu</s>. The views of <s>parakīyā</s> (paramour love) and <s>svakīyā</s> (wedded love) of <s>Śrīmad Rūpa</s> and others — <s>Jīva</s> wrote in his own works regarding both the manifest and unmanifest [pastimes]. Therein, the <s>svakīyā</s> view was not accepted by <s>Śrīmad Raghunātha Dāsa</s> and other associates of <s>Śrī Caitanya</s> who were companions of <s>Śrī Rūpa</s>. The venerable <s>Jīva</s>'s writing of that was not by his own wish, but by the desire of another (<s>parecchā</s>). By the force of his scholarship, seeing his literary style, learned people accept it. But those who have received the grace of <s>Śrī Mahāprabhu</s> and the grace of <s>Śrī Rūpa</s> and others — they by no means accept it. This propagation of the [<s>svakīyā</s>] view was merely a result of circumstance (<s>kāla-kṛtam</s>). As the saying goes: 'I consider all that to be the work of time' and so forth; and 'Auspicious endeavors face many obstacles' and so forth.
